      <label class="lbl">Paste AI responses here</label>
      <div class="field-note">Paste the AI's full copied response into Set 1. If you ask the AI to make questions in smaller groups, paste each extra group into Set 2, Set 3, or Set 4. Launch Quiz will put all filled sets together.</div>
      <div class="paste-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="AI response sets">
        <button class="paste-tab active" id="pasteTab1" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="pastePanel1" onclick="showPasteSetTab(1)">Set 1</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab2" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel2" onclick="showPasteSetTab(2)">Set 2</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab3" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel3" onclick="showPasteSetTab(3)">Set 3</button>
        <button class="paste-tab" id="pasteTab4" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="pastePanel4" onclick="showPasteSetTab(4)">Set 4</button>
      </div>
      <div class="paste-panels">
        <div class="paste-panel active" id="pastePanel1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab1">
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea1">Set 1</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your first AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea1" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 1…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab2" hidden="">
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea2">Set 2</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your second AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea2" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 2…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab3" hidden="">
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea3">Set 3</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your third AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea3" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 3…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="paste-panel" id="pastePanel4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="pasteTab4" hidden="">
          <label class="lbl" for="pasteArea4">Set 4</label>
          <div class="field-note">Use this for your fourth AI batch.</div>
          <textarea class="ta" id="pasteArea4" placeholder="Paste AI response for Set 4…" oninput="syncPasteSetTabs()"></textarea>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="setup-actions">
      <button class="launch-btn" onclick="launchQuiz()">🚀 Launch Quiz</button>
      <button class="ghost-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">⬇ Download My Quiz</button>
    </div>
    <div class="err-msg" id="errMsg"></div>
  </div>

</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════ QUIZ VIEW ══════════ -->
<div class="view" id="viewQuiz">
<div class="quiz-page">

  <!-- top bar -->
  <div class="qt-bar">
    <div class="qt-name" id="qtName">Quiz</div>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtClassicUiBtn" onclick="switchQuizUI('nbme')">⇄ NBME UI</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtMarkBtn" onclick="toggleMark()">⚑ Mark</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" id="qtSubmitBtn" onclick="submitExam()">🧾 Submit Exam</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">⬇ Download Quiz</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="resetQuiz()">↺ Reset</button>
    <button class="qt-btn" onclick="exitQuiz()">✕ Close</button>
  </div>

  <!-- grid -->
  <div class="qin">

    <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
    <div class="sidebar">

      <!-- score boxes -->
      <div class="score-panel">
        <div class="score-box correct"><div class="sv" id="sC">0</div><div class="sl">Correct</div></div>
        <div class="score-box incorrect"><div class="sv" id="sI">0</div><div class="sl">Incorrect</div></div>
        <div class="score-box"><div class="sv" id="sP">—</div><div class="sl">Score</div></div>
      </div>

      <!-- mode toggle -->
      <div class="mode-toggle">
        <button class="mode-btn active" id="modeLearn" onclick="setMode('learn')"><span class="mode-icon">📚</span>Learn Mode</button>
        <button class="mode-btn" id="modeExam" onclick="setMode('exam')"><span class="mode-icon">📝</span>Exam Mode</button>
      </div>

      <!-- timer -->
      <div class="timer-card" id="timerCard">
        <div class="timer-lbl" id="timerModeLbl" onclick="toggleTimerDisplay()" title="Click to switch between elapsed and remaining time">TIMER · ELAPSED</div>
        <div class="timer-time" id="timerTime">00:00</div>
        <button class="tbtn" id="timerPlayBtn" onclick="toggleTimer()">▶</button>
        <button class="tbtn" onclick="resetTimer()">↺</button>
      </div>

      <!-- tools -->
      <div class="tool-panel">
        <button class="q-tool hl-tool" id="hlTool" onclick="toggleTool('hl')">🖊 Highlight</button>
        <button class="q-tool st-tool" id="stTool" onclick="toggleTool('st')">✂ Strikethrough</button>
        <button class="q-tool" onclick="clearMarks()">✖ Clear Marks</button>
        <button class="q-tool lab-tool" onclick="openLabs()">🧪 Lab Values</button>
        <div class="font-tools">
          <span class="font-tools-label">Text Size</span>
          <div class="font-tools-group">
            <button class="font-btn" onclick="adjustFontSize(-1)" aria-label="Decrease question and answer text size">A-</button>
            <button class="font-btn reset" onclick="resetFontSize()" aria-label="Reset question and answer text size">A</button>
            <button class="font-btn" onclick="adjustFontSize(1)" aria-label="Increase question and answer text size">A+</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- RIGHT: QUESTION -->
    <div>
      <div id="finBanner"></div>
      <div id="qArea"></div>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════ NBME / NBOME VIEW ══════════ -->
<div class="view" id="viewQuizNbme">
<div class="nb-wrap">
  <div class="nb-util">
    <div class="nb-util-name" id="nbQuizTitle">Quiz</div>
    <div class="nb-util-actions">
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="toggleFullscreen()" id="nbFsBtn">⛶ Fullscreen</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="switchQuizUI('classic')">UNE UI</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="downloadQuiz()">Download</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="resetQuiz()">Reset</button>
      <button class="nb-util-btn" onclick="exitQuiz()">Close</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="nb-app">
    <header class="nb-header">
      <div class="nb-status-box">
        <strong id="nbItemIndicator">Question: 1 of 1</strong>
        <span id="nbStatusMeta">Answered 0 of 1</span>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-header-center">
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbMarkBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleMark()">⚑ Mark</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbHighlightBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleTool('hl')">Highlight</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbStrikeBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleTool('st')">Strikeout</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" type="button" onclick="clearMarks()">Clear</button>
        <button class="nb-mode-pill active" id="nbModeLearn" type="button" onclick="setMode('learn')">Learn</button>
        <button class="nb-mode-pill" id="nbModeExam" type="button" onclick="setMode('exam')">Testing</button>
        <button class="nb-action-btn" id="nbSummaryBtn" type="button" onclick="toggleSummaryPanel()" disabled="">See Score &amp; Summary</button>
        <div class="nb-item-picker">
          <label for="nbItemSelect">Question</label>
          <select id="nbItemSelect" aria-label="Jump to a question" onchange="goQ(parseInt(this.value,10))"></select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-nav-controls">
        <button class="nb-nav-btn" id="nbPrevBtn" type="button" onclick="prevQ()">
          <span class="icon">◀</span><span>Previous</span>
        </button>
        <button class="nb-nav-btn" id="nbNextBtn" type="button" onclick="nextQ()">
          <span class="icon">▶</span><span>Next</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="nb-body">
      <aside class="nb-rail" aria-label="Question navigator">
        <div class="nb-rail-title">Questions</div>
        <button class="nb-rail-arrow" id="nbRailUpBtn" type="button" aria-label="Scroll question list up" onclick="scrollNbmeRail(-1)">▲</button>
        <div class="nb-rail-scroll" id="nbRailScroll" onscroll="syncNbmeRailArrows()">
          <div id="nbRailItems"></div>
        </div>
        <button class="nb-rail-arrow" id="nbRailDownBtn" type="button" aria-label="Scroll question list down" onclick="scrollNbmeRail(1)">▼</button>
      </aside>

      <section class="nb-stage">
        <div id="nbFinishBanner"></div>
        <div class="nb-question-scroll">
          <div id="nbContent"></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="nb-footer">
      <div class="nb-timer-box" id="nbTimerCard">
        <button class="nb-timer-mode" id="nbTimerModeLbl" type="button" onclick="toggleTimerDisplay()" title="Click to switch between elapsed and remaining time">Elapsed Time:</button><strong id="nbTimerTime">00:00</strong>
        <span>Status:</span><strong id="nbTimerStatus">Paused</strong>
        <small id="nbTimerNote">Same timer as the original interface</small>
        <div class="nb-timer-actions">
          <button class="nb-timer-mini" id="nbTimerToggle" type="button" onclick="toggleTimer()">Start</button>
          <button class="nb-timer-mini" type="button" onclick="resetTimer()">Reset</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-footer-center">
        <div class="nb-score-chip">
          <span>Correct <strong id="nbScoreCorrect">0</strong></span>
          <span>Incorrect <strong id="nbScoreIncorrect">0</strong></span>
          <span>Score <strong id="nbScorePercent">—</strong></span>
        </div>
        <button class="nb-footer-tool" type="button" onclick="openLabs()">▤ Lab Values</button>
        <div class="nb-font-cluster">
          <label>Text</label>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="adjustFontSize(-1)">A-</button>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="resetFontSize()">A</button>
          <button class="nb-font-btn" type="button" onclick="adjustFontSize(1)">A+</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="nb-end-wrap">
        <button class="nb-end-btn" id="nbReviewBtn" type="button" onclick="handleNbmeEndAction()">Testing Mode Only</button>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="lab-modal" id="labModal" onclick="if(event.target===this)closeLabs()">
  <div class="lab-shell">
    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-header">
        <div>
          <div class="lab-kicker">Reference Table</div>
          <h2 class="lab-title">Laboratory Values</h2>
          <div class="lab-sub">Shared lab reference for both quiz interfaces, imported from the NBME-style simulator so the values stay consistent.</div>
        </div>
        <button class="lab-close" type="button" onclick="closeLabs()">Close</button>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-controls">
        <div class="lab-toggle">
          <button class="lab-toggle-btn active" id="labUnitsUs" type="button" onclick="setLabUnits('us')">U.S. Units</button>
          <button class="lab-toggle-btn" id="labUnitsSi" type="button" onclick="setLabUnits('si')">SI Units</button>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-note">Switch units without leaving the question.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div id="labTables"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
// ── STATE ──
var questions=[],questionBank=[],answers={},selected={},marked={},stemMarkup={},choiceMarkup={},NC=0,NI=0,CQ=0;
var quizMode='learn';
var uiMode='classic';
var examSubmitted=false;
var reviewOpen=false;
var summaryVisible=false;
var finishBannerMinimized=false;
var toolMode='';
var highlightColor='#fff173', annotationRange=null, annotationRoot=null, suppressChoiceUntil=0;
var labUnitMode='us';
var timerSec=0,timerRunning=false,timerInterval=null;
var fontStep=0;
var LETTERS=['A','B','C','D','E','F','G','H'];
var MAX_ANSWER_CHOICES=8;
var PRELOADED_QUIZ_NAME="Anemia II: Hemolytic Anemias";
var PRELOADED_QUESTIONS_JSON="[{\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital due to fatigue, dark urine, and jaundice for the past 2 days. She has no chronic medical conditions and takes no daily medications. Temperature is 37.8 C (100 F) and pulse is 104/min. Physical examination shows scleral icterus; the abdomen is soft and the spleen is not palpable. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.6 g/dL, hematocrit 23%, mean corpuscular volume 92 fL, and reticulocyte count 9%. Total bilirubin is 4.2 mg/dL with a direct fraction of 0.4 mg/dL and lactate dehydrogenase is markedly elevated. Serum haptoglobin is undetectable (normal: 30-200 mg/dL). Spun plasma appears pink, and urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood with no erythrocytes on microscopy. Which of the following best explains this patient's undetectable serum haptoglobin?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Consumption during reticuloendothelial clearance of hemoglobin complexes\",\"B. Decreased hepatic synthesis in the setting of cirrhosis\",\"C. Increased glomerular filtration of unbound haptoglobin\",\"D. Reduced synthesis as a negative acute phase reactant\",\"E. Sequestration within an enlarged spleen\"],\"correct\":0,\"explanation\":\"Pink plasma, a urine dipstick positive for blood without erythrocytes, and an undetectable haptoglobin identify intravascular hemolysis. Haptoglobin is a plasma protein whose sole job is to bind free hemoglobin released when erythrocytes rupture inside the vessel -> the hemoglobin-haptoglobin complex is cleared by macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system -> haptoglobin is consumed along with it -> the serum level falls. Haptoglobin is therefore a consumption marker, not a production marker, which is why it falls further in intravascular hemolysis, where far more free hemoglobin must be mopped up, than in extravascular hemolysis, where it is only mildly decreased.\\nEducational objective: Serum haptoglobin falls because it is consumed while clearing free plasma hemoglobin, not because synthesis decreases. It is profoundly low or absent in intravascular hemolysis (pink plasma, hemoglobinuria) and only mildly decreased in extravascular hemolysis, where destruction occurs inside splenic macrophages.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"1\":\"Decreased hepatic synthesis lowers haptoglobin in advanced cirrhosis, alongside hypoalbuminemia, a prolonged prothrombin time, and stigmata of chronic liver disease. This patient has an isolated indirect hyperbilirubinemia with no evidence of hepatic synthetic failure.\",\"2\":\"Haptoglobin itself is too large to pass the glomerular filtration barrier and is not lost in the urine even in nephrotic syndrome, where albumin and smaller proteins are. What appears in this patient's urine is free hemoglobin that exceeded the binding capacity of haptoglobin.\",\"3\":\"Negative acute phase reactants such as albumin and transferrin fall during inflammation; haptoglobin is a positive acute phase reactant and rises in inflammation, which can mask hemolysis in a septic patient. Inflammation would therefore raise, not lower, this value.\",\"4\":\"Splenic sequestration traps intact cells and produces cytopenias with marked splenomegaly, as in hypersplenism from portal hypertension. This patient's spleen is not palpable, and sequestered cells are not lysed within the vessel.\"},\"eli5\":\"Free hemoglobin loose in the blood is poisonous, so the body keeps a protein that grabs it and hauls it away. Every time it grabs some, that protein is used up and thrown out too. So when the blood test shows almost none of it left, a lot of red cells have burst.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 44-year-old man is admitted to the hospital due to dark urine and jaundice for the past 24 hours. Three days ago he began trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a lower extremity cellulitis; he takes no other medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and a resolving area of erythema on the left calf; there is no rash, lymphadenopathy, or costovertebral angle tenderness. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL, hematocrit 24%, reticulocyte count 8%, and a serum creatinine that has risen from 0.9 to 2.6 mg/dL. Spun plasma is red-tinged, urine dipstick is positive for blood with no erythrocytes on microscopy, and the eosinophil count is normal. Peripheral smear shows erythrocytes with semicircular defects at the cell margin. Which of the following best explains this patient's rising serum creatinine?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Antibody-mediated injury of the glomerular basement membrane\",\"B. Deposition of immune complexes within the mesangium\",\"C. Direct tubular injury by filtered free heme\",\"D. Interstitial inflammation from a drug hypersensitivity reaction\",\"E. Obstruction of tubules by uric acid crystals\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"An oxidant sulfonamide triggered hemolysis in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency; the semicircular defects are bite cells, produced when splenic macrophages remove Heinz bodies with the overlying membrane. Intravascular rupture -> free hemoglobin in plasma (red-tinged plasma) -> filtration of hemoglobin and heme at the glomerulus -> direct oxidative and obstructive injury to tubular epithelium -> acute kidney injury. Hemoglobin is essential inside the erythrocyte and toxic outside it, which is why intravascular hemolysis is the more dangerous form and why the same mechanism kills patients after ABO-incompatible transfusion.\\nEducational objective: Free plasma hemoglobin released during intravascular hemolysis is filtered at the glomerulus and is directly nephrotoxic to tubular epithelium, causing acute kidney injury. Extravascular hemolysis, in which macrophages dismantle hemoglobin internally, releases nothing free into plasma and does not threaten the kidney.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Antibody directed against the glomerular basement membrane causes anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, presenting with hematuria, dysmorphic erythrocytes and red cell casts, and often pulmonary hemorrhage. This patient's dipstick is positive for heme with no erythrocytes on microscopy, which is hemoglobinuria rather than hematuria.\",\"1\":\"Mesangial immune complex deposition characterizes IgA nephropathy, which classically produces gross hematuria within days of a mucosal infection. Red cells would be visible on microscopy, and there would be no hemolysis or red-tinged plasma.\",\"3\":\"Acute interstitial nephritis is a genuine adverse effect of sulfonamides and typically appears with fever, rash, eosinophilia, and sterile pyuria with white cell casts. This patient has none of those findings, and interstitial nephritis does not cause hemolysis.\",\"4\":\"Uric acid crystal obstruction causes acute kidney injury in tumor lysis syndrome, following treatment of a bulky, rapidly dividing malignancy, with hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypocalcemia. There is no malignancy or recent cytotoxic therapy here.\"},\"eli5\":\"Hemoglobin does its job safely while it is locked inside a red blood cell. When cells burst open in the bloodstream, that hemoglobin spills out and gets filtered by the kidneys, where it poisons the little tubes. That is why bursting inside the vessels is much more dangerous than being eaten by the spleen.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is evaluated for an episode of jaundice and dark urine that began 2 days after he started nitrofurantoin for a urinary tract infection and resolved on its own within a week. He has had two similar episodes in the past, each following a febrile illness. An erythrocyte enzyme assay obtained 3 months after full recovery shows markedly reduced activity, and genetic testing confirms an X-linked variant. Deficiency of this enzyme most directly impairs which of the following reactions?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to 6-phosphogluconolactone\",\"B. Conversion of hydrogen peroxide to water by glutathione peroxidase\",\"C. Conversion of methemoglobin to hemoglobin by cytochrome b5 reductase\",\"D. Conversion of oxidized glutathione to reduced glutathione\",\"E. Conversion of superoxide to hydrogen peroxide by superoxide dismutase\",\"F. Generation of adenosine triphosphate by anaerobic glycolysis\"],\"correct\":0,\"explanation\":\"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) catalyzes the first and rate-limiting step of the hexose monophosphate shunt, oxidizing glucose-6-phosphate to 6-phosphogluconolactone and generating NADPH. In an erythrocyte this is the only meaningful source of NADPH -> NADPH powers glutathione reductase to regenerate reduced glutathione -> reduced glutathione lets glutathione peroxidase convert hydrogen peroxide to water. Without the first step the whole chain fails, hydrogen peroxide accumulates, hemoglobin is oxidized and precipitates as Heinz bodies, and splenic macrophages bite out the inclusions. Because a mature erythrocyte has no nucleus or mitochondria, it can neither make replacement enzyme nor use an alternative NADPH route.\\nEducational objective: G6PD catalyzes the conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to 6-phosphogluconolactone, the erythrocyte's only source of NADPH. NADPH regenerates reduced glutathione, which detoxifies hydrogen peroxide; without it, oxidant stress from infection, sulfonamides, antimalarials, or fava beans precipitates hemoglobin as Heinz bodies.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"1\":\"Glutathione peroxidase performs the final detoxifying step of the pathway and fails secondarily here, because it lacks reduced glutathione rather than because the enzyme itself is defective. Primary glutathione peroxidase deficiency is a rare disorder distinct from this X-linked condition.\",\"2\":\"Cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency causes congenital methemoglobinemia, presenting with cyanosis unresponsive to supplemental oxygen and chocolate-brown blood, treated with methylene blue. This patient has episodic hemolysis rather than persistent cyanosis.\",\"3\":\"Glutathione reductase performs the step immediately downstream, using the NADPH that G6PD supplies; it is the adjacent link in the same chain rather than the deficient enzyme. Its activity is normal when substrate is available.\",\"4\":\"Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide and is the enzyme implicated in familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis; it generates the peroxide rather than removing it. Its loss would not produce oxidant-triggered hemolysis.\",\"5\":\"Impaired anaerobic glycolysis describes pyruvate kinase deficiency, an autosomal recessive chronic hemolytic anemia present from birth without oxidant triggers. This patient is well between exposures, which points to an antioxidant defect rather than an energy defect.\"},\"eli5\":\"Red blood cells have exactly one way to make the chemical that neutralizes damaging oxygen molecules, and this enzyme is the first step of it. Break that first step and the whole cleanup crew stops working. Then anything oxidizing, like certain drugs or an infection, wrecks the hemoglobin inside.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man is admitted to the hospital due to fatigue, jaundice, and dark urine for 1 day. He was diagnosed with community-acquired pneumonia 4 days ago and is being treated with azithromycin. Temperature is 38.4 C (101.1 F) and pulse is 108/min. Physical examination shows scleral icterus and crackles at the right base; the spleen is not palpable. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL, hematocrit 25%, reticulocyte count 7%, elevated lactate dehydrogenase, and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. A Wright-stained peripheral smear shows scattered erythrocytes with a semicircular portion of the cell margin missing, and supravital staining with crystal violet shows multiple dark, round inclusions adherent to the inner erythrocyte membrane. The inclusions seen on supravital staining are most likely composed of which of the following?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Aggregated ribosomal RNA\",\"B. Denatured, oxidized hemoglobin\",\"C. Iron-containing granules within siderosomes\",\"D. Remnants of the mitotic spindle apparatus\",\"E. Residual nuclear DNA\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"Infection is the commonest trigger of hemolysis in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. Oxidant stress -> hydrogen peroxide accumulates -> hemoglobin is oxidized, denatures, and precipitates against the inner membrane as a Heinz body. Heinz bodies are invisible on a routine Wright stain and require a supravital stain such as crystal violet. A splenic macrophage recognizes the abnormal inclusion and removes it together with the overlying membrane, leaving a bite cell; when the membrane remains stretched over the defect, the result is a blister cell.\\nEducational objective: Heinz bodies are precipitates of denatured, oxidized hemoglobin visible only with supravital staining, and their removal by splenic macrophages produces bite and blister cells. They are characteristic of G6PD deficiency and also occur in thalassemia, where unpaired globin chains precipitate.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Aggregated ribosomal RNA produces basophilic stippling, which is visible on a routine Wright stain and points to lead poisoning or thalassemia. Diffuse stippling of reticulocytes with supravital stain is likewise RNA, but it does not form discrete membrane-bound inclusions.\",\"2\":\"Iron granules within siderosomes form Pappenheimer bodies, seen in sideroblastic anemia and after splenectomy and confirmed with a Prussian blue stain. They appear on routine staining and are not associated with bite cells.\",\"3\":\"Mitotic spindle remnants form Cabot rings, thin looped structures reported in megaloblastic anemia and severe hemolysis. They are ring-shaped rather than dense round inclusions and are not generated by oxidant stress.\",\"4\":\"Residual nuclear DNA forms Howell-Jolly bodies, which indicate that the spleen is absent or non-functioning, as in sickle cell autosplenectomy or after splenectomy. This patient's spleen is clearly working, since it is producing bite cells.\"},\"eli5\":\"When hemoglobin gets chemically burned, it clumps into a lump that sticks to the inside of the red cell wall. The spleen spots the lump and bites that piece out of the cell. The leftover cell looks like someone took a bite out of a cookie.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man is admitted to the hospital due to fatigue, jaundice, and tea-colored urine for 2 days, which began during treatment of an acute febrile illness. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows scleral icterus; there is no splenomegaly or lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL, hematocrit 24%, mean corpuscular volume 96 fL, and reticulocyte count 12%. Lactate dehydrogenase is elevated, haptoglobin is undetectable, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. The peripheral smear shows erythrocytes with semicircular marginal defects. An erythrocyte glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase activity assay drawn on the day of admission is within the reference range. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in establishing the diagnosis?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy\",\"B. Direct antiglobulin testing with anti-complement reagent\",\"C. Hemoglobin electrophoresis\",\"D. Measurement of erythrocyte enzyme activity 3 months after recovery\",\"E. Measurement of erythrocyte osmotic fragility\"],\"correct\":3,\"explanation\":\"In glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency the enzyme is made in normal amounts but is unstable, so activity decays as the cell ages and only older erythrocytes are vulnerable. During a crisis those old, enzyme-depleted cells are precisely the ones destroyed, and the compensatory reticulocytosis floods the sample with young cells carrying the highest enzyme content of any erythrocyte -> the measured average can be falsely normal. The assay must be repeated 2-3 months after the episode, once the cell age distribution has returned to baseline; genetic testing is unaffected by timing.\\nEducational objective: A G6PD enzyme assay drawn during acute hemolysis can be falsely normal, because the most deficient older cells have already been destroyed and reticulocytes carry the highest enzyme levels. Repeat the assay 2-3 months after recovery, or send genetic testing, which is not timing dependent.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Marrow aspiration and biopsy are reserved for suspected primary marrow disease such as unexplained pancytopenia, leukemia, or myelodysplasia. This patient has a brisk reticulocytosis, which proves the marrow is intact and responding.\",\"1\":\"Direct antiglobulin testing with an anti-complement reagent identifies cold agglutinin disease, in which cells are coated with C3 after a Mycoplasma or Epstein-Barr virus infection. This patient's direct antiglobulin test is already negative, excluding immune-mediated hemolysis.\",\"2\":\"Hemoglobin electrophoresis establishes sickle cell disease and the thalassemias by separating hemoglobin species. It is normal in an enzyme deficiency, and this patient's mean corpuscular volume of 96 fL argues against a globin chain disorder.\",\"4\":\"Increased osmotic fragility supports hereditary spherocytosis, in which cells that are already spheres lyse in hypotonic saline where biconcave discs do not. The smear here shows bite cells rather than spherocytes, and the presentation is episodic rather than lifelong.\"},\"eli5\":\"This enzyme wears out as a red cell gets older, so only the old cells are in trouble. During an attack, the old cells are already destroyed and the body sends out brand-new ones packed with fresh enzyme. Test at that moment and the result looks falsely fine, so you wait a few months and test again.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman comes to the office for preconception counseling. Her brother was hospitalized at age 6 due to jaundice, pallor, and dark urine 2 days after a family meal that included fava beans, and molecular testing confirmed an erythrocyte enzyme deficiency; her mother carries the same variant. The patient has never had jaundice or anemia, and targeted testing shows that she carries one copy of the variant allele. Her partner has no personal or family history of hemolytic anemia and testing shows no variant allele. Vital signs are within normal limits and physical examination is normal. Which of the following best represents the probability that a son born to this couple will be affected?\",\"choices\":[\"A. 0%\",\"B. 25%\",\"C. 50%\",\"D. 75%\",\"E. 100%\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency is X-linked, which is why it is expressed most fully in males. This woman is a heterozygous carrier with one variant X and one normal X. A son receives his single X from his mother and his Y from his father -> each son has a 1 in 2 chance of inheriting the variant X -> 50% of sons are affected. Daughters each have a 50% chance of being carriers but, with an unaffected father, none will be homozygous.\\nEducational objective: In X-linked recessive inheritance, a carrier mother transmits the variant allele to half of her sons, who are affected, and to half of her daughters, who become carriers. Fathers cannot transmit an X-linked allele to their sons, so an unaffected father does not change a son's risk.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"A 0% risk would apply if the mother carried no variant allele, so that neither X she could transmit was affected. Targeted testing has confirmed that she carries one copy.\",\"1\":\"A 25% risk per pregnancy is the figure for an autosomal recessive condition with two carrier parents, such as beta thalassemia or sickle cell disease. It is also the risk of an affected son among all pregnancies here, but the question asks the probability among sons specifically.\",\"3\":\"A 75% figure corresponds to the chance of being unaffected in an autosomal recessive cross of two carriers and does not arise from X-linked transmission from a single heterozygous mother.\",\"4\":\"A 100% risk to sons would require the mother to be homozygous for the variant, that is, clinically affected herself. This patient has never had hemolysis and carries only one variant allele.\"},\"eli5\":\"Boys get exactly one X chromosome, and it always comes from their mother. Mom has one normal X and one with the change, so each son flips a coin. That makes the chance one in two.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old woman comes to the office due to fatigue and intermittent yellowing of her eyes since childhood. Her father had similar episodes as a teenager and was never given a diagnosis; her two siblings are asymptomatic. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows mild pallor and moderate scleral icterus, with the spleen palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin and no hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.2 g/dL, hematocrit 31%, mean corpuscular volume 88 fL, mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration 37.5 g/dL, and reticulocyte count 8%. Total bilirubin is 4.6 mg/dL with a direct fraction of 0.5 mg/dL, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. Peripheral smear shows dense round erythrocytes that lack central pallor. A defect in which of the following proteins is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Ankyrin\",\"B. Dystrophin\",\"C. Fibrillin-1\",\"D. Glycoprotein Ib\",\"E. Type IV collagen\",\"F. von Willebrand factor\"],\"correct\":0,\"explanation\":\"Autosomal dominant transmission, lifelong jaundice, splenomegaly, an elevated mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, reticulocytosis, spherocytes, and a negative direct antiglobulin test identify hereditary spherocytosis. The defect lies in the membrane cytoskeleton, most often ankyrin, and also band 3, alpha or beta spectrin, or protein 4.2 (genes ANK1, SPTB, SLC4A1). Loss of the lattice that clips the membrane to the cytoskeleton -> the cell rounds up into a sphere -> minimal surface area for its volume and poor deformability -> it cannot traverse the splenic cords -> extravascular destruction by splenic macrophages.\\nEducational objective: Hereditary spherocytosis is an autosomal dominant defect of erythrocyte membrane cytoskeletal proteins (ankyrin, band 3, spectrin, protein 4.2) causing spherocytes with an elevated mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration and extravascular hemolysis. A negative direct antiglobulin test separates it from warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which also produces spherocytes.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"1\":\"Dystrophin links the muscle fiber cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix, and its loss causes Duchenne muscular dystrophy, presenting with proximal weakness, calf pseudohypertrophy, and an elevated creatine kinase. It is not an erythrocyte membrane protein.\",\"2\":\"Fibrillin-1 forms the microfibrillar scaffold of elastic tissue, and its mutation causes Marfan syndrome with aortic root dilation, ectopia lentis, and a tall thin habitus. Hemolysis is not part of that disorder.\",\"3\":\"Glycoprotein Ib is the platelet receptor for von Willebrand factor; its deficiency causes Bernard-Soulier syndrome with giant platelets and mucocutaneous bleeding. This patient has hemolysis rather than a platelet disorder.\",\"4\":\"Type IV collagen forms the glomerular basement membrane, and its mutation causes Alport syndrome with hematuria, sensorineural hearing loss, and lenticonus. Erythrocyte shape is unaffected.\",\"5\":\"Deficiency or dysfunction of von Willebrand factor causes the most common inherited bleeding disorder, with mucocutaneous bleeding and an abnormal ristocetin cofactor activity. It plays no role in erythrocyte membrane integrity.\"},\"eli5\":\"A normal red blood cell keeps its flat, bendy doughnut shape because of a protein scaffold clipped to the inside of its skin. If one of those scaffold proteins is faulty, the cell balls up like a marble. Marbles cannot squeeze through the spleen's narrow filters, so the spleen destroys them.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and vomiting for the past 6 hours. He has had intermittent scleral icterus since childhood and reports that his mother had her spleen removed as a young adult. Temperature is 38.1 C (100.6 F). Physical examination shows scleral icterus, right upper quadrant tenderness with inspiratory arrest on palpation, and the spleen palpable 4 cm below the left costal margin. Right upper quadrant ultrasonography shows multiple small echogenic gallstones with a thickened gallbladder wall. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL, hematocrit 32%, reticulocyte count 7%, total bilirubin 3.8 mg/dL with a direct fraction of 0.6 mg/dL, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase. The peripheral smear shows dense round erythrocytes lacking central pallor, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. This patient's gallstones are most likely composed of which of the following?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Calcium bilirubinate\",\"B. Calcium oxalate\",\"C. Cholesterol monohydrate\",\"D. Magnesium ammonium phosphate\",\"E. Uric acid\"],\"correct\":0,\"explanation\":\"Lifelong jaundice, splenomegaly, reticulocytosis, spherocytes, and a negative direct antiglobulin test in a patient with an affected parent indicate hereditary spherocytosis. Chronic extravascular hemolysis -> continuous heme catabolism -> a sustained unconjugated bilirubin load delivered to the biliary tree -> precipitation of calcium bilirubinate as black pigment stones. Pigmented gallstones at an unusually young age are a signature of chronic hemolysis and should prompt a search for the underlying red cell disorder; the full chronic extravascular quartet is anemia, jaundice, splenomegaly, and pigment stones.\\nEducational objective: Chronic hemolysis delivers a sustained unconjugated bilirubin load to the biliary tract and produces black pigment (calcium bilirubinate) gallstones, often in adolescence or early adulthood. Cholesterol stones, by contrast, arise from biliary cholesterol supersaturation and are associated with obesity, female sex, and rapid weight loss.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"1\":\"Calcium oxalate forms the most common kidney stone, favored by hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria as in Crohn disease or after bariatric surgery, and low urine volume. It does not form in the biliary tree.\",\"2\":\"Cholesterol monohydrate stones are the most common gallstone overall and follow biliary cholesterol supersaturation, with risk from obesity, female sex, pregnancy, and rapid weight loss. They would not be expected in a lean 24-year-old man with ongoing hemolysis.\",\"3\":\"Magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite) stones are urinary calculi that form in alkaline urine during infection with urease-producing organisms such as Proteus, often filling the renal pelvis as staghorn calculi.\",\"4\":\"Uric acid stones are radiolucent urinary calculi arising in persistently acidic urine and in high cell turnover states such as tumor lysis and myeloproliferative disease. Hemolysis raises bilirubin rather than depositing urate in the gallbladder.\"},\"eli5\":\"Every red cell that gets destroyed releases a yellow pigment the liver has to dump into bile. If cells are being destroyed all the time, that pigment piles up and hardens into little stones in the gallbladder. That is why a young person with stones may really have a blood problem.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis is evaluated before elective total splenectomy. He has required three hospital admissions for symptomatic anemia in the past 2 years, and the spleen is palpable 8 cm below the left costal margin. He has no chronic medical conditions other than his anemia, takes folic acid daily, and his childhood immunizations are up to date. Vital signs are within normal limits. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, hematocrit 28%, and reticulocyte count 9%. He is counseled that removing the site of destruction will normalize erythrocyte lifespan although the spherocytes will persist, and that the procedure carries a permanent infectious risk. At least 2 weeks before the operation, immunization against which of the following organisms is most important?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Bordetella pertussis\",\"B. Clostridium tetani\",\"C. Corynebacterium diphtheriae\",\"D. Listeria monocytogenes\",\"E. Neisseria gonorrhoeae\",\"F. Streptococcus pneumoniae\"],\"correct\":5,\"explanation\":\"The spleen clears opsonized encapsulated bacteria from the bloodstream, a function no other organ replaces. Splenectomy or functional asplenia -> impaired clearance of polysaccharide-encapsulated organisms -> risk of fulminant sepsis within hours. Streptococcus pneumoniae is by far the leading cause of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection, followed by Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae type b; vaccination against all three should be given at least 2 weeks preoperatively, with daily antibiotic prophylaxis in children.\\nEducational objective: Asplenic patients cannot clear encapsulated organisms and are at lifelong risk of fulminant sepsis, most often from Streptococcus pneumoniae. Vaccination against pneumococcus, meningococcus, and Haemophilus influenzae type b should precede elective splenectomy by at least 2 weeks.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Bordetella pertussis causes whooping cough and is vaccinated against routinely in childhood and with each pregnancy. It is not encapsulated in the manner that requires splenic clearance, and asplenia does not raise the risk.\",\"1\":\"Clostridium tetani produces tetanospasmin, which blocks release of glycine and GABA from spinal inhibitory interneurons, and immunization is given as routine boosters and wound prophylaxis. Protection depends on antitoxin antibody rather than splenic clearance.\",\"2\":\"Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes pharyngeal pseudomembrane and myocarditis through an ADP-ribosylating exotoxin, and vaccination is toxoid based. It is not an encapsulated organism cleared by the spleen.\",\"3\":\"Listeria monocytogenes causes meningitis in neonates, pregnant women, older adults, and patients with impaired cell-mediated immunity; there is no vaccine, and prevention relies on food precautions. Defense depends on T cells and macrophages rather than the spleen.\",\"4\":\"Neisseria gonorrhoeae lacks a polysaccharide capsule, has no available vaccine, and causes urethritis, cervicitis, and disseminated arthritis-dermatitis. Its sibling Neisseria meningitidis is the encapsulated species relevant after splenectomy.\"},\"eli5\":\"The spleen is the body's main filter for bacteria wrapped in a slippery sugar coat, which the immune system struggles to grab on its own. Take the spleen out and those particular germs can go from a sniffle to life-threatening within hours. So you get the shots first.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old woman is evaluated for a 4-month history of fatigue and intermittent scleral icterus. She has no chronic medical conditions and takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor, scleral icterus, and the spleen palpable 4 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL, hematocrit 29%, mean corpuscular volume 87 fL, and reticulocyte count 9%. Total bilirubin is 3.9 mg/dL with a direct fraction of 0.4 mg/dL and lactate dehydrogenase is elevated. Peripheral smear shows dense round erythrocytes lacking central pallor with no fragmented forms, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?\\n\\n     Spun plasma appearance | Serum haptoglobin | Urine free hemoglobin\",\"choices\":[\"A. Pink | Absent | Absent\",\"B. Pink | Absent | Present\",\"C. Pink | Normal | Present\",\"D. Yellow | Absent | Present\",\"E. Yellow | Mildly decreased | Absent\",\"F. Yellow | Normal | Absent\"],\"correct\":4,\"explanation\":\"Spherocytes without fragments, splenomegaly, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, and reticulocytosis indicate extravascular hemolysis. The cell is removed intact by a splenic macrophage, which dismantles hemoglobin internally -> nothing free is released into plasma -> the plasma stays straw-yellow and no hemoglobin reaches the urine. Some hemoglobin still leaks, so haptoglobin is mildly decreased rather than absent. Intravascular hemolysis produces the opposite triad: pink plasma, hemoglobinuria, and an undetectable haptoglobin, with schistocytes rather than spherocytes and a normal-sized spleen.\\nEducational objective: Extravascular hemolysis produces spherocytes, splenomegaly, and only a mild decrease in haptoglobin, with normal-appearing plasma and no hemoglobinuria. Intravascular hemolysis produces pink plasma, hemoglobinuria, an absent haptoglobin, and a risk of heme-induced acute kidney injury.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Pink plasma with an absent haptoglobin and no urinary hemoglobin describes early or modest intravascular hemolysis, in which free hemoglobin is present in plasma but has not yet exceeded the renal threshold. This patient has no free plasma hemoglobin at all, since destruction occurs inside macrophages.\",\"1\":\"Pink plasma, an absent haptoglobin, and hemoglobinuria constitute the full intravascular triad, seen after ABO-incompatible transfusion, in severe G6PD crises, and in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. Those patients have schistocytes rather than spherocytes and no splenomegaly.\",\"2\":\"Free plasma hemoglobin sufficient to tint the plasma and spill into the urine could not coexist with a normal haptoglobin, because haptoglobin is consumed first as it binds hemoglobin. This row is internally inconsistent for any real hemolytic process.\",\"3\":\"Hemoglobinuria with an absent haptoglobin but normal-appearing plasma is not a coherent pattern, since hemoglobin must be free in plasma before it can be filtered. Whenever the urine contains free hemoglobin, the plasma is discolored as well.\",\"5\":\"Entirely normal plasma, haptoglobin, and urine would indicate no hemolysis at all, as in anemia from impaired production, where the reticulocyte count is low. This patient's reticulocyte count of 9% with an elevated lactate dehydrogenase and indirect bilirubin proves cells are being destroyed.\"},\"eli5\":\"There are two ways red cells can die: burst open inside the blood vessels, or be quietly eaten whole by the spleen. If they burst, hemoglobin spills everywhere and turns the plasma pink and the urine dark. If the spleen eats them, everything is tidied up inside and the plasma stays yellow.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy with sickle cell disease is brought to the emergency department due to increasing pallor and fatigue for the past 3 days. He had a week of low-grade fever and mild headache that resolved before the pallor began. His baseline hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL. Temperature is 37.1 C (98.8 F) and pulse is 122/min. Physical examination shows marked pallor and mild scleral icterus; there is no splenomegaly, and there is no bone pain or chest pain. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 4.6 g/dL, hematocrit 14%, platelet count 260,000/mm3, and reticulocyte count 0.3%. Total bilirubin is near his baseline value. The organism most likely responsible for this presentation binds to which of the following molecules on erythroid cells?\",\"choices\":[\"A. CD21\",\"B. CD4\",\"C. Decay-accelerating factor (CD55)\",\"D. Glycophorin A\",\"E. P antigen (globoside)\",\"F. Transferrin receptor (CD71)\"],\"correct\":4,\"explanation\":\"A sudden hemoglobin crash with a reticulocyte count that falls rather than rises, in a patient whose survival depends on a permanently maximal marrow, defines an aplastic crisis. Parvovirus B19 attaches to the P antigen (globoside) on erythroid progenitors -> lytic infection of red cell precursors -> erythropoiesis ceases for roughly a week -> hemoglobin plummets while reticulocytes disappear. The bilirubin stays near baseline because the rate of destruction has not changed; production has stopped. The same mechanism produces aplastic crisis in hereditary spherocytosis and other chronic hemolytic states.\\nEducational objective: Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors by binding the erythrocyte P antigen and transiently halts erythropoiesis. In chronic hemolysis (eg, sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) this causes an aplastic crisis: a precipitous hemoglobin fall with a low reticulocyte count.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"CD21 (complement receptor 2) is the B lymphocyte receptor for Epstein-Barr virus gp350, producing infectious mononucleosis with pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, and atypical lymphocytes. It is not expressed on erythroid precursors.\",\"1\":\"CD4 is the receptor for HIV gp120 on helper T lymphocytes, monocytes, and macrophages. HIV causes anemia through marrow suppression and chronic disease rather than an abrupt reticulocytopenic crash after a viral prodrome.\",\"2\":\"Decay-accelerating factor is a glycosylphosphatidylinositol-anchored complement regulator that is absent in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, in which complement lyses unprotected cells and causes intravascular hemolysis with thrombosis. That produces hemoglobinuria with a high reticulocyte count, not reticulocytopenia.\",\"3\":\"Glycophorin A is used by Plasmodium falciparum merozoites to invade mature erythrocytes, producing malaria with cyclical fevers and hemolysis. Malaria destroys circulating cells and drives the reticulocyte count up.\",\"5\":\"The transferrin receptor delivers iron to developing erythroblasts and is central to hemoglobin synthesis, not to viral attachment. Interfering with iron delivery produces a microcytic anemia, not an abrupt aplastic crisis.\"},\"eli5\":\"Someone with sickle cell disease is destroying red cells constantly, so their bone marrow has to run at full speed just to keep up. A common childhood virus can switch that marrow off for about a week by attacking the baby red cells. In a normal person that is harmless; in this child the blood count falls off a cliff.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, is evaluated at 30 weeks gestation due to a fundal height greater than expected for dates. Both she and her partner are known to carry a two-gene deletion of the alpha globin locus, and their first pregnancy ended in intrauterine fetal demise at 32 weeks. Maternal vital signs are within normal limits. Fetal ultrasonography shows generalized subcutaneous edema, ascites, a pericardial effusion, cardiomegaly, and a thickened placenta. Fetal blood sampling shows profound anemia, with hemoglobin composed almost entirely of gamma globin tetramers and no detectable alpha chain-containing species. Which of the following best explains the fetal tissue hypoxia in this pregnancy?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Failure of the perinatal switch from gamma to beta globin synthesis\",\"B. Increased oxygen affinity preventing release of oxygen to tissue\",\"C. Intramedullary destruction of erythroid precursors by unpaired chains\",\"D. Polymerization of hemoglobin under conditions of low oxygen tension\",\"E. Precipitation of excess beta globin chains within circulating erythrocytes\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"Deletion of all four alpha globin genes leaves no alpha chain to partner with gamma, so excess gamma chains assemble into hemoglobin Barts (gamma4). Hemoglobin Barts has an extremely high oxygen affinity -> it binds oxygen avidly and will not release it at the tissue level -> the fetus is profoundly hypoxic despite being full of hemoglobin -> high-output failure with generalized edema, effusions, and a thickened placenta (hydrops fetalis), and intrauterine death without prenatal intervention. Because alpha chains are required by both fetal and adult hemoglobin, alpha thalassemia, unlike beta thalassemia, manifests in utero.\\nEducational objective: Deletion of all four alpha globin genes produces hemoglobin Barts (gamma4), whose extremely high oxygen affinity prevents oxygen delivery to tissue and causes hydrops fetalis. Alpha thalassemia presents in utero because alpha chains are needed for fetal hemoglobin, whereas beta thalassemia presents at 3-6 months, after the globin switch.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Failure to complete the gamma-to-beta switch describes hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin, a benign condition, and is also the reason beta thalassemia major becomes apparent at 3-6 months of age. It does not cause fetal disease, since a fetus normally runs on fetal hemoglobin.\",\"2\":\"Intramedullary destruction of precursors by precipitated unpaired chains is ineffective erythropoiesis, the dominant mechanism in beta thalassemia major, producing a hypercellular marrow with profound anemia and marrow cavity expansion. It does not by itself impair oxygen unloading.\",\"3\":\"Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin describes sickle cell disease, in which the substituted valine exposes a hydrophobic surface and rigid fibers deform the cell. Sickle cell disease also spares the fetus, because HbS requires beta chains.\",\"4\":\"Precipitation of excess beta chains as hemoglobin H (beta4) occurs when a single alpha gene remains, producing a moderately severe hemolytic anemia compatible with life. This fetus has no alpha genes and forms gamma tetramers instead.\"},\"eli5\":\"Hemoglobin has to grab oxygen in the lungs and then let it go in the body. With no alpha chains at all, the baby makes a strange hemoglobin that grabs oxygen and refuses to release it. The blood is full of oxygen the tissues can never use.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the office due to fatigue and poor exercise tolerance that his parents have noticed since he started school. He was born at term and has never required a transfusion. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows mild pallor and scleral icterus, with the spleen palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL, hematocrit 26%, mean corpuscular volume 66 fL, and reticulocyte count 6%. Serum iron, ferritin, and total iron binding capacity are normal. Peripheral smear shows hypochromic microcytic erythrocytes with target cells and marked variation in size and shape. Molecular testing shows deletion of three of the four alpha globin genes. Which of the following hemoglobin species is most likely to be increased in this patient?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Hemoglobin A (alpha2 beta2)\",\"B. Hemoglobin A2 (alpha2 delta2)\",\"C. Hemoglobin Barts (gamma4)\",\"D. Hemoglobin F (alpha2 gamma2)\",\"E. Hemoglobin H (beta4)\",\"F. Hemoglobin S (alpha2 betaS2)\"],\"correct\":4,\"explanation\":\"Loss of three of four alpha genes markedly reduces alpha chain synthesis, leaving beta chains without a partner. Excess beta chains assemble into tetramers of hemoglobin H (beta4) -> HbH is unstable, is oxidized, and precipitates as inclusions -> splenic macrophages remove the affected cells, producing a moderately severe hemolytic anemia with microcytosis, target cells, and splenomegaly. Normal iron studies with a disproportionately low mean corpuscular volume distinguish thalassemia from iron deficiency, and giving iron to such a patient produces only iron overload.\\nEducational objective: Deletion of three of four alpha globin genes causes hemoglobin H disease, in which unpartnered beta chains form beta4 tetramers that precipitate and drive splenic hemolysis. Deletion of all four genes instead yields hemoglobin Barts (gamma4) and hydrops fetalis.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Hemoglobin A (alpha2 beta2) is the normal adult species and is reduced, not increased, when alpha chain synthesis is deficient. Its absolute reduction is precisely why unpartnered beta chains accumulate.\",\"1\":\"An elevated hemoglobin A2 (alpha2 delta2), along with elevated hemoglobin F, is the electrophoretic signature of beta thalassemia, in which beta chains are the deficient species. Both require alpha chains and are therefore reduced in alpha thalassemia.\",\"2\":\"Hemoglobin Barts (gamma4) forms in the fetus when gamma chains lack alpha partners, and is present in small amounts on newborn screening in alpha thalassemia trait and hemoglobin H disease. It disappears after infancy because gamma chain synthesis ceases; it is not increased in a 6-year-old.\",\"3\":\"Hemoglobin F (alpha2 gamma2) is elevated in beta thalassemia and is raised therapeutically by hydroxyurea in sickle cell disease. It contains alpha chains and cannot accumulate when alpha synthesis is the limiting step.\",\"5\":\"Hemoglobin S results from a missense mutation in the beta globin gene and is detected in sickle cell trait and disease. This patient has a quantitative alpha chain deletion, not a structural beta chain variant.\"},\"eli5\":\"Hemoglobin normally pairs two kinds of chains together, like partners in a dance. If one kind is in short supply, the leftover partners clump together into a group of four that does not work properly. Those clumps damage the cell, and the spleen throws it away.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 3-year-old boy with beta thalassemia major is admitted to the hospital for a scheduled transfusion. He has received packed red blood cells every 4 weeks since age 1. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows pallor, mild scleral icterus, and the liver and spleen palpable 3 and 6 cm below the costal margins; height and weight are below the fifth percentile. Laboratory studies before transfusion show hemoglobin 6.5 g/dL, hematocrit 20%, mean corpuscular volume 63 fL, and reticulocyte count 4%. Lactate dehydrogenase is elevated and unconjugated bilirubin is increased. A bone marrow biopsy performed during an earlier evaluation showed a markedly hypercellular marrow with striking erythroid hyperplasia. Which of the following best explains the severity of this patient's anemia despite the hypercellular marrow?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Antibody-mediated destruction of circulating erythrocytes\",\"B. Impaired erythropoietin response to tissue hypoxia\",\"C. Intramedullary destruction of precursors by precipitated globin chains\",\"D. Iron sequestration within reticuloendothelial macrophages\",\"E. Mechanical fragmentation of erythrocytes in the microvasculature\",\"F. Splenic sequestration of mature erythrocytes\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"In beta thalassemia major the deficiency of beta chains leaves a relative excess of structurally normal alpha chains with nothing to pair with. Free alpha chains are highly unstable -> they aggregate into insoluble inclusions inside developing erythroblasts -> precursors are destroyed within the marrow before release, which is ineffective erythropoiesis -> the marrow is packed with cells while the reticulocyte count remains inappropriately low for the degree of anemia. Cells that do escape carry inclusions and are removed by the spleen. The severity of thalassemia tracks the chain made in excess, not merely the chain that is missing, which is why free alpha chains make beta thalassemia worse than comparable alpha gene loss.\\nEducational objective: In thalassemia the unpartnered globin chain precipitates and kills erythroid precursors within the marrow, producing ineffective erythropoiesis: a hypercellular marrow with profound anemia and an inappropriately low reticulocyte count. Severity tracks the chain produced in excess rather than the one that is deficient.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Antibody-mediated destruction defines autoimmune hemolytic anemia, in which the direct antiglobulin test is positive and spherocytes appear on the smear. It destroys mature circulating cells, so the reticulocyte response is brisk rather than blunted.\",\"1\":\"An impaired erythropoietin response underlies the anemia of chronic kidney disease and contributes in anemia of chronic disease; the marrow is then normocellular or hypocellular because the stimulus to build is absent. This marrow is markedly hypercellular, proving the drive is intact.\",\"3\":\"Iron sequestration in macrophages is the hepcidin-driven mechanism of anemia of chronic disease, with a high ferritin, a low total iron binding capacity, and a mildly microcytic or normocytic anemia. This patient has a globin synthesis defect, and his iron burden comes from transfusion rather than sequestration.\",\"4\":\"Mechanical fragmentation produces schistocytes in microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and around prosthetic valves, always with a compensatory reticulocytosis. No fragmented cells are described here.\",\"5\":\"Splenic sequestration traps intact cells and can cause an abrupt hemoglobin fall with a rapidly enlarging spleen, as in sickle cell sequestration crisis. It would not produce a hypercellular marrow with intramedullary cell death.\"},\"eli5\":\"Hemoglobin needs two kinds of protein chains in equal numbers. When one kind is missing, the extra chains have no partner, so they clump up and poison the cells being built in the bone marrow. The factory is crammed with workers, but almost nothing makes it out the door.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 5-year-old boy with transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia major is brought to the office for routine follow-up. He has received packed red blood cells every 3 to 4 weeks since infancy and takes deferasirox and folic acid. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows pallor, prominent frontal bones and cheekbones with an overbite, and the spleen palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin; height and weight are below the fifth percentile. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.0 g/dL and mean corpuscular volume 64 fL, with elevated lactate dehydrogenase and unconjugated bilirubin. Radiographs of the skull show widening of the diploic space with perpendicular trabeculae, and radiographs of the hands show thinned cortices with coarse trabeculation. Which of the following best explains this patient's craniofacial findings?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Deficient bone mineralization from vitamin D deficiency\",\"B. Expansion of medullary cavities by hyperplastic marrow\",\"C. Extramedullary hematopoiesis within the liver and spleen\",\"D. Osteonecrosis from microvascular occlusion\",\"E. Subperiosteal bone resorption from secondary hyperparathyroidism\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"Ineffective erythropoiesis in beta thalassemia major creates an enormous, unrelenting erythropoietic drive. Chronic anemia and intramedullary precursor death -> maximal marrow stimulation -> hyperplastic marrow expands into every available space, widening the diploic space of the skull and the maxilla and thinning the cortex of tubular bones -> frontal bossing, maxillary prominence, and pathologic fracture risk. The same drive diverts calories from growth, producing failure to thrive. Adequate transfusion suppresses the drive and is what prevents these skeletal changes.\\nEducational objective: Chronic ineffective erythropoiesis in beta thalassemia major drives marrow hyperplasia that expands the medullary cavities of the skull and face, producing frontal bossing, maxillary prominence, and a widened diploic space. Adequate transfusion suppresses the erythropoietic drive and prevents these skeletal changes.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Deficient mineralization from vitamin D deficiency causes rickets, with bowing of the legs, widened wrists, a rachitic rosary, and a low calcium and phosphate with a high alkaline phosphatase. It does not widen the diploic space or expand the maxilla.\",\"2\":\"Extramedullary hematopoiesis genuinely occurs in thalassemia and contributes to hepatosplenomegaly and occasional paraspinal masses. It represents blood formation outside bone and therefore does not explain expansion of the skull and facial bones themselves.\",\"3\":\"Osteonecrosis from vaso-occlusion is characteristic of sickle cell disease, affecting the femoral and humeral heads and producing dactylitis in infants. Thalassemia does not cause vaso-occlusion.\",\"4\":\"Subperiosteal resorption, a salt-and-pepper skull, and brown tumors indicate hyperparathyroidism, typically secondary to chronic kidney disease, with an elevated parathyroid hormone and phosphate. This patient has normal renal function and a hematologic cause for his bone changes.\"},\"eli5\":\"When the bone marrow is desperate to make red cells, it grows and pushes outward against the bone around it. In the skull and face there is not much room, so the bones bulge and the cheekbones stick out. Regular transfusions calm the marrow down and stop this from happening.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man is evaluated for a mild anemia found on a routine health maintenance examination. He is asymptomatic, has no chronic medical conditions, and takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits and physical examination is normal. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 11.6 g/dL, hematocrit 35%, mean corpuscular volume 67 fL, and an erythrocyte count that is elevated for the degree of anemia; serum iron, ferritin, and total iron binding capacity are normal and the red cell distribution width is normal. Peripheral smear shows numerous erythrocytes with a central rounded density surrounded by a pale ring, with hemoglobin also concentrated at the cell periphery. Which of the following best explains the morphology of these erythrocytes?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Decreased ratio of membrane surface area to cell volume\",\"B. Increased ratio of membrane surface area to cell volume\",\"C. Loss of membrane fragments during splenic transit\",\"D. Oxidative denaturation of intracellular hemoglobin\",\"E. Polymerization of hemoglobin at low oxygen tension\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"Microcytosis with a disproportionately high erythrocyte count, normal iron studies, and a normal red cell distribution width indicate thalassemia trait. Deficient globin synthesis -> too little hemoglobin for the amount of membrane the cell carries -> an increased surface area-to-volume ratio -> the excess membrane redistributes so that hemoglobin piles up centrally and around the rim, separated by a pale ring, producing the target cell (codocyte). Target cells also occur in liver disease, hemoglobin C, iron deficiency, and after splenectomy, all situations of relative membrane excess.\\nEducational objective: Target cells arise when the ratio of membrane surface area to hemoglobin content increases, so excess membrane redistributes into a central density surrounded by a pale ring. They occur in thalassemia, liver disease, hemoglobin C disease, iron deficiency, and asplenia.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"A decreased surface area-to-volume ratio describes the spherocyte, in which membrane has been lost or the cytoskeleton is defective and the cell rounds up with no central pallor at all. That morphology points to hereditary spherocytosis or warm autoimmune hemolysis.\",\"2\":\"Loss of membrane during splenic transit also produces spherocytes, either after macrophages strip antibody-coated membrane or after they bite out a Heinz body. This patient's cells have too much membrane rather than too little.\",\"3\":\"Oxidative denaturation of hemoglobin creates Heinz bodies and, after splenic bites, bite and blister cells, as in G6PD deficiency after an oxidant exposure. This patient is asymptomatic with no oxidant trigger.\",\"4\":\"Polymerization of deoxygenated hemoglobin S forms rigid fibers that deform the cell into a crescent, the hallmark of sickle cell disease. Sickled cells are elongated and pointed rather than target shaped.\"},\"eli5\":\"Picture a balloon skin with only a little air inside: there is too much skin for the contents, so it sags and folds. A red cell short on hemoglobin does the same thing, and the extra membrane makes the middle look like a bullseye.\"},{\"stem\":\"A newborn screening specimen obtained at 36 hours of life from a term male infant shows hemoglobin F 78%, hemoglobin A 16%, and hemoglobin Barts 6%, with no hemoglobin S or C detected. The infant is well appearing, is feeding normally, and has a normal physical examination. He is followed in the office, and at age 9 months he is asymptomatic with normal growth. Laboratory studies at that visit show hemoglobin 11.4 g/dL, hematocrit 34%, mean corpuscular volume 68 fL, and a red cell distribution width within the reference range; serum iron, ferritin, and total iron binding capacity are normal, and hemoglobin electrophoresis shows a normal pattern with no hemoglobin Barts. Deletion of how many alpha globin genes is most likely present in this infant?\",\"choices\":[\"A. 0\",\"B. 1\",\"C. 2\",\"D. 3\",\"E. 4\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"Every person has four alpha globin genes, two on each copy of chromosome 16, and the clinical grade tracks how many remain. Hemoglobin Barts at birth quantifies the alpha deficit, since gamma chains that cannot find an alpha partner form gamma4. A Barts fraction of about 5%-6% at birth with later mild microcytosis, a normal red cell distribution width, and normal iron studies corresponds to alpha thalassemia trait, that is, two genes deleted. The clinical importance is negative: this microcytosis must not be treated as iron deficiency, since iron supplementation achieves nothing except iron loading.\\nEducational objective: The severity of alpha thalassemia tracks the number of deleted alpha globin genes: three remaining is a silent carrier, two is alpha thalassemia trait with microcytosis and normal iron studies, one is hemoglobin H disease, and none causes hydrops fetalis. Microcytosis with normal iron studies should not be treated with iron.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"No gene deletion is expected in a child with a completely normal newborn screen and a normal mean corpuscular volume; hemoglobin Barts would be absent. This infant has both a measurable Barts fraction and persistent microcytosis.\",\"1\":\"Deletion of one gene leaves a silent carrier, who has a trace of hemoglobin Barts at birth of roughly 1%-2% and entirely normal indices thereafter. This infant's mean corpuscular volume of 68 fL is too low for a silent carrier.\",\"3\":\"Deletion of three genes causes hemoglobin H disease, with 20%-30% hemoglobin Barts at birth and a lifelong moderately severe hemolytic anemia with splenomegaly and target cells. This infant is asymptomatic with a hemoglobin of 11.4 g/dL.\",\"4\":\"Deletion of all four genes prevents any alpha chain synthesis, producing hemoglobin Barts hydrops fetalis with intrauterine anasarca and fetal demise without prenatal intervention. Such an infant does not present as a well newborn.\"},\"eli5\":\"You get four copies of one hemoglobin gene, and how sick you are depends on how many are missing. Missing two means slightly small red cells and no symptoms at all. The important part is not to mistake it for low iron and hand out iron pills that will not help.\"},{\"stem\":\"A newborn screening specimen from a term female infant shows hemoglobin F 82% and hemoglobin S 18%, with no hemoglobin A detected. She was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy, and her physical examination at 2 weeks of age is normal. Her parents report no family history of hemolytic anemia; both are asymptomatic. Confirmatory hemoglobin electrophoresis at 3 months of age shows hemoglobin S 74%, hemoglobin F 24%, and hemoglobin A 0%, and she is enrolled in a comprehensive care program with daily penicillin prophylaxis. Which of the following best describes the molecular abnormality underlying this infant's condition?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Deletion of two of four alpha globin genes\",\"B. Frameshift mutation producing a truncated beta globin chain\",\"C. Missense mutation replacing glutamic acid with valine in beta globin\",\"D. Promoter mutation reducing transcription of the beta globin gene\",\"E. Splice site mutation causing retention of an intron in beta globin messenger RNA\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"Hemoglobin S with a complete absence of hemoglobin A indicates homozygous sickle cell disease rather than trait. A single adenine-to-thymine substitution in the sixth codon of the beta globin gene (HBB) replaces the charged, hydrophilic glutamic acid with hydrophobic valine. Deoxygenation shifts the molecule's conformation and exposes that hydrophobic surface -> hemoglobin S molecules polymerize into long rigid fibers -> the cell deforms into a crescent and becomes inflexible -> it lodges in the microvasculature, causing ischemia, pain, and hemolysis. In trait, roughly 40%-45% of hemoglobin is S with the remainder A; the absence of any hemoglobin A is what proves homozygosity.\\nEducational objective: Sickle cell disease results from a missense mutation substituting valine for glutamic acid at the sixth residue of beta globin, creating a hydrophobic patch that polymerizes when deoxygenated. Electrophoresis showing hemoglobin S with no hemoglobin A establishes homozygous disease rather than trait.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Deletion of two alpha globin genes causes alpha thalassemia trait, with mild microcytosis, normal iron studies, and a small hemoglobin Barts fraction at birth. Alpha thalassemia is a quantitative defect and does not produce a structurally abnormal hemoglobin band.\",\"1\":\"Frameshift and nonsense mutations that abolish beta chain output cause beta-zero thalassemia, presenting at 3-6 months with severe transfusion-dependent anemia and elevated hemoglobin F and A2. That is a defect of quantity rather than structure.\",\"3\":\"Promoter mutations that reduce but do not abolish beta globin transcription cause beta-plus thalassemia, with a milder anemia and some residual hemoglobin A. Any residual hemoglobin A would be visible on electrophoresis, and none is present here.\",\"4\":\"Splice site mutations causing intron retention are another mechanism of beta thalassemia, producing unstable transcripts and reduced beta chain output. Again the result is too little normal hemoglobin, not an abnormally migrating hemoglobin S band.\"},\"eli5\":\"One letter in the DNA recipe for hemoglobin is swapped, so one building block is replaced by a greasy one. Greasy patches stick to each other, so the hemoglobin molecules glue into stiff rods when oxygen is low. Those rods bend the red cell into a hard crescent that jams in small blood vessels.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man with sickle cell disease is brought to the emergency department due to severe pain in the lower back, hips, and thighs for the past 6 hours. He completed a road race in hot weather earlier that day and drank little fluid during or after the event. Temperature is 37.4 C (99.3 F), blood pressure is 104/62 mm Hg, and pulse is 118/min. Physical examination shows dry mucous membranes and diffuse tenderness over the lumbar spine and both thighs; the chest is clear and there is no focal neurologic deficit. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL, which is near his baseline, reticulocyte count 8%, serum bicarbonate 17 mEq/L, and an elevated serum lactate. Which of the following changes most directly promotes polymerization of hemoglobin S in this patient?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Decreased intracellular hemoglobin concentration\",\"B. Decreased proportion of hemoglobin S\",\"C. Increased intracellular hemoglobin concentration\",\"D. Increased intracellular pH\",\"E. Increased oxygen saturation of hemoglobin\",\"F. Increased proportion of fetal hemoglobin\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"Three variables govern how much hemoglobin S polymerizes: the proportion of hemoglobin S relative to species it polymerizes poorly with (HbF and HbA), the intracellular hemoglobin concentration, and pH. Dehydration -> water leaves the erythrocyte -> intracellular hemoglobin becomes more concentrated -> molecules collide more often and polymerize faster. The concurrent metabolic acidosis compounds this by lowering hemoglobin's oxygen affinity through the Bohr effect, favoring the deoxygenated conformation that polymerizes. This is why intravenous fluids and oxygen are the backbone of acute management and why hydroxyurea and transfusion, which dilute hemoglobin S, are the disease-modifying therapies.\\nEducational objective: Polymerization of hemoglobin S is promoted by a higher intracellular hemoglobin concentration, acidosis, deoxygenation, and a higher hemoglobin S fraction. Treatment reverses each variable: hydration dilutes the cell, oxygen keeps hemoglobin in the non-polymerizing conformation, and hydroxyurea and transfusion lower the hemoglobin S fraction.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"A lower intracellular hemoglobin concentration is achieved with intravenous fluids and is therapeutic, since polymerization depends on how crowded the hemoglobin molecules are. It is the intervention rather than the trigger.\",\"1\":\"A lower hemoglobin S fraction is exactly what transfusion and exchange transfusion accomplish, and randomized data show that keeping hemoglobin S under 30% substantially reduces stroke risk. Lowering it inhibits rather than promotes polymerization.\",\"3\":\"An increased intracellular pH raises hemoglobin's oxygen affinity, keeping more molecules in the oxygenated conformation that does not polymerize. This patient's bicarbonate of 17 mEq/L indicates the opposite, an acidosis.\",\"4\":\"Higher oxygen saturation keeps hemoglobin S in the conformation that tucks away the hydrophobic valine, which is why supplemental oxygen is supportive therapy. Polymerization requires deoxygenation.\",\"5\":\"A higher fetal hemoglobin fraction is protective, since hemoglobin S polymerizes poorly with hemoglobin F; raising it is the mechanism by which hydroxyurea reduces crises. It cannot be the driver of an acute crisis.\"},\"eli5\":\"Sickle hemoglobin only clumps together when the cells are crowded, acidic, and short of oxygen. Running hard in the heat without drinking makes the inside of every red cell more concentrated and more acidic at once. That is a perfect recipe for the hemoglobin to stiffen into rods.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy with sickle cell disease is evaluated in the hematology clinic. Annual transcranial Doppler ultrasonography shows a time-averaged mean cerebral blood flow velocity of 210 cm/s in the right middle cerebral artery, a value associated with a high risk of first ischemic stroke. He has had three vaso-occlusive crises in the past year and takes hydroxyurea and folic acid. Vital signs are within normal limits and neurologic examination is normal. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.6 g/dL, hematocrit 26%, and a hemoglobin S fraction of 82%. A chronic transfusion program targeting a hemoglobin S fraction below 30% is planned, and erythrocytapheresis rather than simple transfusion is chosen. Which of the following best describes the reason for choosing exchange transfusion in this patient?\",\"choices\":[\"A. It avoids alloimmunization to minor erythrocyte antigens\",\"B. It eliminates the future need for iron chelation therapy\",\"C. It increases fetal hemoglobin production by erythroid precursors\",\"D. It lowers the hemoglobin S fraction without raising total blood volume\",\"E. It provides transfused erythrocytes with a longer circulating lifespan\"],\"correct\":3,\"explanation\":\"The target is a hemoglobin S fraction below 30%, the threshold shown in randomized data to reduce stroke risk substantially. Reaching that target from 82% by simple transfusion alone would require an impossible transfused volume and would drive the hematocrit to dangerously hyperviscous levels, which in sickle cell disease itself promotes vaso-occlusion. Exchange transfusion removes the patient's own sickle-containing blood while donor blood is infused -> the hemoglobin S percentage falls sharply -> total blood volume and hematocrit remain acceptable. It is a lifelong commitment once begun.\\nEducational objective: Exchange transfusion lowers the hemoglobin S fraction without raising total blood volume or hematocrit, which simple transfusion cannot do when the starting hemoglobin S percentage is high. Maintaining hemoglobin S below 30% substantially reduces the risk of first ischemic stroke in children with abnormal transcranial Doppler velocities.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Alloimmunization to minor Rh, Kell, and other antigens is a recognized hazard of chronic transfusion and is mitigated by extended phenotype matching, not by exchange. Exchange transfusion exposes the patient to more donor units, if anything increasing that risk.\",\"1\":\"Exchange transfusion reduces but does not eliminate iron accumulation, because donor erythrocytes are still infused and the body has no route to excrete iron. Chronically transfused patients require monitoring and often chelation with deferasirox regardless of technique.\",\"2\":\"Increasing fetal hemoglobin production is the mechanism of hydroxyurea, which triggers stress erythropoiesis favoring hemoglobin F; its principal toxicity is myelosuppression. Transfusion dilutes hemoglobin S from outside rather than reprogramming the marrow.\",\"4\":\"Transfused donor erythrocytes have the same finite storage-limited survival regardless of whether they are given by simple or exchange transfusion. The benefit of exchange lies in what is removed, not in the durability of what is given.\"},\"eli5\":\"To protect his brain, doctors need most of his hemoglobin to be the normal kind, not the sickle kind. Just adding more blood would make his blood too thick before it ever got there. So they take some of his blood out while putting donor blood in, which swaps the bad for the good without overfilling him.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman with sickle cell disease comes to the office due to progressive shortness of breath on exertion for the past 8 months. She now becomes breathless walking up a single flight of stairs, which she managed easily a year ago. She has had chronic ulcers over both medial malleoli for 2 years. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows scleral icterus, a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a right ventricular heave, and shallow ulcers over both ankles; the lungs are clear and there is no peripheral edema. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, reticulocyte count 11%, markedly elevated lactate dehydrogenase, and undetectable haptoglobin. Echocardiography shows an elevated estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure with normal left ventricular size and systolic and diastolic function. Which of the following best explains this patient's pulmonary vascular findings?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Chronic hypoxemia from recurrent pulmonary infarction\",\"B. Depletion of nitric oxide by free plasma hemoglobin\",\"C. Elevated left atrial pressure from diastolic dysfunction\",\"D. Iron deposition within pulmonary arteriolar walls\",\"E. Thromboembolism arising from a deep venous source\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"The markedly elevated lactate dehydrogenase with an undetectable haptoglobin marks a high rate of intravascular hemolysis. Free hemoglobin released into plasma avidly scavenges nitric oxide and also releases arginase, which depletes the substrate for nitric oxide synthesis -> loss of endothelium-derived vasodilation and increased reactive oxygen species -> a vasculopathy phenotype comprising pulmonary hypertension, leg ulcers, priapism, and cerebrovascular disease. This hemolysis-driven arm of sickle cell disease is distinct from the vaso-occlusive pain arm, and normal left heart filling on echocardiography excludes a postcapillary cause.\\nEducational objective: Free plasma hemoglobin released during intravascular hemolysis scavenges nitric oxide and impairs endothelium-dependent vasodilation, producing the hemolysis-associated vasculopathy of sickle cell disease: pulmonary hypertension, leg ulcers, priapism, and stroke. This arm is distinct from the vaso-occlusive pain phenotype.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Chronic hypoxemia causes hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and cor pulmonale, as in advanced chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or obstructive sleep apnea. This patient's lungs are clear, and the mechanism described here operates through nitric oxide rather than alveolar hypoxia.\",\"2\":\"Elevated left atrial pressure produces postcapillary pulmonary hypertension in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction and mitral valve disease. Echocardiography here shows normal left ventricular size and diastolic function, excluding a left-sided cause.\",\"3\":\"Iron deposition damages the heart, liver, and endocrine organs in transfusional iron overload and hereditary hemochromatosis, presenting with cardiomyopathy, cirrhosis, and diabetes. It does not selectively remodel pulmonary arterioles, and this patient has no transfusion history described.\",\"4\":\"Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension follows unresolved pulmonary emboli and is suggested by segmental perfusion defects on ventilation-perfusion scanning. There is no history of venous thromboembolism, and it would not explain the leg ulcers.\"},\"eli5\":\"Blood vessels stay relaxed thanks to a gas called nitric oxide made by their lining. When red cells burst, the hemoglobin that spills out soaks up that gas like a sponge. The vessels then stay tight, which raises the pressure in the lungs and starves the skin of the ankles.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old boy with sickle cell disease is admitted to the hospital due to severe pain in both arms and the lower back, and is treated with intravenous fluids and opioids. On hospital day 2 he develops fever and pleuritic chest pain. Temperature is 38.7 C (101.7 F), respirations are 28/min, and oxygen saturation is 88% on room air, decreased from 97% on admission. Physical examination shows decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion at the left base; the neck veins are flat and there is no peripheral edema or leg swelling. Chest radiography shows a new consolidation in the left lower lobe that was not present on admission. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 6.4 g/dL, decreased from 8.9 g/dL on admission, platelet count 190,000/mm3, and reticulocyte count 10%; prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's pulmonary findings?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Alveolar hemorrhage from consumptive coagulopathy\",\"B. Deposition of immune complexes within alveolar capillaries\",\"C. Hydrostatic pulmonary edema from transfusion-associated volume overload\",\"D. Increased capillary permeability from donor antileukocyte antibodies\",\"E. Pulmonary microvascular occlusion by sickled erythrocytes with infarction\"],\"correct\":4,\"explanation\":\"Fever, chest pain, hypoxemia, and a new pulmonary infiltrate developing during a vaso-occlusive crisis define acute chest syndrome, a leading cause of hospitalization and death in sickle cell disease. Hypoventilation from chest and back pain plus opioid therapy -> regional hypoxemia -> deoxygenated hemoglobin S polymerizes in the pulmonary microvasculature -> occlusion, infarction, and inflammation -> worsening hypoxemia, which propagates further sickling in a self-amplifying cycle. Infection and fat embolism from infarcted marrow are common co-triggers. Management is oxygen, antibiotics, analgesia adequate to permit deep breathing, incentive spirometry, and transfusion.\\nEducational objective: Acute chest syndrome is a new pulmonary infiltrate with fever and respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease, caused by microvascular occlusion and infarction that hypoxemia propagates in a self-amplifying cycle. It is a leading cause of death and requires oxygen, antibiotics, analgesia, and transfusion.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"Alveolar hemorrhage from disseminated intravascular coagulation would be accompanied by a low platelet count, prolonged prothrombin and activated partial thromboplastin times, a low fibrinogen, and an elevated D-dimer. This patient's platelet count and coagulation times are normal.\",\"1\":\"Immune complex deposition in alveolar capillaries characterizes pulmonary vasculitis and pulmonary-renal syndromes, which present with hemoptysis, glomerulonephritis, and positive serologies over weeks. That tempo does not fit an infiltrate appearing within 24 hours of a pain crisis.\",\"2\":\"Transfusion-associated circulatory overload causes hydrostatic edema with hypertension, distended neck veins, and an elevated brain natriuretic peptide, typically within hours of a transfusion. This patient's neck veins are flat and no transfusion has been given.\",\"3\":\"Donor antileukocyte antibodies cause transfusion-related acute lung injury, with hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates within 6 hours of a transfusion and a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. Again, no transfusion has been administered.\"},\"eli5\":\"Chest pain makes you breathe shallowly, and pain medicine makes that worse, so part of the lung gets less oxygen. Low oxygen is exactly what makes sickle cells stiffen and clog. They block the small lung vessels, oxygen drops further, and the whole thing feeds on itself.\"},{\"stem\":\"Researchers are studying childhood mortality in a region of sub-Saharan Africa where Plasmodium falciparum transmission is intense and year-round. Among 12,400 children followed from birth to age 5, those heterozygous for a point mutation in the beta globin gene have approximately 30% lower malaria-attributable mortality than children with two normal beta globin alleles and are otherwise clinically well. Children homozygous for the same mutation have markedly increased mortality from severe anemia, infection, and vaso-occlusive complications. The allele frequency in this population has remained stable across several generations, and the geographic distribution of the allele closely overlaps historical malaria endemicity. Which of the following best explains the persistence of this allele at high frequency in this population?\",\"choices\":[\"A. Founder effect within a geographically isolated population\",\"B. Genetic drift acting on a selectively neutral allele\",\"C. Heterozygote advantage under malarial selection pressure\",\"D. Increased mutation rate at the beta globin locus\",\"E. Nonrandom mating with respect to carrier status\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"An allele that is lethal in homozygotes would ordinarily be purged from a population. It persists when heterozygotes have higher fitness than either homozygote, a state called balanced polymorphism or heterozygote advantage. Sickle cell trait reduces malaria mortality by up to 30% -> carriers survive and reproduce preferentially in malarious regions -> the allele is maintained despite the severe disease in homozygotes. The overlap between the geographic distribution of the allele and historical malaria endemicity is the population-level evidence; glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency and the thalassemias show the same pattern.\\nEducational objective: Alleles that are deleterious in homozygotes can persist at high frequency when heterozygotes have a survival advantage (balanced polymorphism). Sickle cell trait, G6PD deficiency, and the thalassemias are maintained in historically malarious regions by protection against Plasmodium falciparum.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"A founder effect explains high allele frequency when a small group establishes a population, as with Tay-Sachs disease in Ashkenazi Jewish communities or Ellis-van Creveld syndrome among the Amish. It does not require, or explain, a fitness benefit to carriers, and this allele is common across many unrelated populations sharing only malaria exposure.\",\"1\":\"Genetic drift changes allele frequencies randomly and predominates in small populations for alleles with no effect on fitness. Neither condition holds here: the population is large, and the allele has strong opposing effects on survival in carriers and homozygotes.\",\"3\":\"An elevated mutation rate could in principle sustain an allele, but human point mutation rates are far too low to offset the loss of homozygotes, and the sickle mutation arose on a limited number of ancestral haplotypes rather than recurrently at high rate.\",\"4\":\"Nonrandom mating, such as consanguinity, increases the proportion of homozygotes without changing allele frequency; it would accelerate loss of a lethal recessive allele rather than preserve it. Some communities in fact promote premarital carrier testing, which reduces affected births.\"},\"eli5\":\"Carrying one copy of the sickle gene makes malaria much less deadly, but carrying two copies causes serious disease. In places where malaria kills many children, the one-copy people survive best, so the gene keeps getting passed on. Evolution is trading one danger for another.\"},{\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man with sickle cell disease comes to the emergency department due to severe pain in both hips and the lower back for the past 10 hours, similar to his previous crises. This is his eighth visit this year for pain. Temperature is 37.2 C (99 F), blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg, and pulse is 104/min. Physical examination shows a man in obvious distress, with tenderness over both hips; the chest is clear and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.1 g/dL, which is near his baseline, and a reticulocyte count of 9%. As the physician begins the evaluation, the patient says, \\\"Every time I come here, someone looks at my chart, sees how often I've been in, and decides I'm just here for the medication. I'm in real pain and nobody believes me.\\\" Which of the following is the most appropriate initial response by the physician?\",\"choices\":[\"A. \\\"I can give you your usual dose today, but we will need to work on spacing these visits out.\\\"\",\"B. \\\"It sounds like you have felt judged when you come here, and that must be exhausting; tell me about the pain you are having right now so we can treat it properly.\\\"\",\"C. \\\"Let me bring in the pain service, since they are the ones who manage patients on long-term opioids.\\\"\",\"D. \\\"Sickle cell pain is real and most patients are not drug-seeking, so please try not to take it personally.\\\"\",\"E. \\\"Your visits have been frequent, so let's start by reviewing how you have been using your medications at home.\\\"\"],\"correct\":1,\"explanation\":\"Undertreatment of pain in sickle cell disease is a well-documented failure of care, and patients frequently encounter suspicion that their pain is drug seeking. This patient has disclosed an emotional injury and is also in acute pain. The response that fits the communication hierarchy names and validates the emotion and, in the same breath, takes the concrete action the patient came for: assessing and treating the crisis. Analgesia is not merely comfort care here, since pain-limited breathing predisposes to acute chest syndrome.\\nEducational objective: When a patient discloses feeling disbelieved or judged, the physician should acknowledge and validate that experience and pair it with a concrete action addressing the patient's immediate concern. Deflecting, defending the system, or making treatment conditional damages the therapeutic alliance and perpetuates undertreatment of sickle cell pain.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"This response makes treatment conditional and immediately imposes the physician's agenda about visit frequency, before the patient's disclosure has been acknowledged or the current crisis assessed. It confirms rather than dispels the patient's sense of being judged.\",\"2\":\"Referring the conversation to the pain service deflects to another team and offloads both the emotional disclosure and the acute crisis in front of the physician. Consultation may have a role later, but it is not an initial response to a patient in acute pain.\",\"3\":\"This response offers generalized reassurance and, by telling the patient not to take it personally, minimizes an experience he has just described as real and repeated. False reassurance closes the conversation instead of exploring it.\",\"4\":\"Opening with the frequency of his visits and a medication review places the physician's concern about opioid use ahead of the patient's stated concern and his untreated pain. Reviewing home analgesic use is legitimate, but not as the first move in an acute crisis.\"},\"eli5\":\"This patient is hurting badly and has also just said that he feels no one believes him. The best first move is to say that you hear him and then get on with treating the pain. Arguing, explaining, or handing him off first tells him he was right not to trust the room.\"},{\"stem\":\"A randomized controlled trial enrolls 260 children with sickle cell disease who have elevated transcranial Doppler cerebral blood flow velocities. Half are assigned to a chronic transfusion program targeting a hemoglobin S fraction below 30% and half to standard care with observation. The groups are similar at baseline in age, hemoglobin concentration, and hydroxyurea use, and follow-up is complete for all participants. Over 2 years, a first ischemic stroke occurs in 1 of 130 children (0.8%) in the transfusion group and in 13 of 130 children (10.0%) in the standard care group. Which of the following is the best estimate of the number of children who must be treated with chronic transfusion for 2 years to prevent one stroke?\",\"choices\":[\"A. 1\",\"B. 10\",\"C. 11\",\"D. 92\",\"E. 130\"],\"correct\":2,\"explanation\":\"The number needed to treat is the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction. Control event rate 10.0% minus experimental event rate 0.8% -> absolute risk reduction 9.2% -> number needed to treat = 1 / 0.092, which is approximately 11. The relative risk reduction here is large (about 92%), but relative measures cannot be converted to a number needed to treat, because they carry no information about baseline risk; the same relative reduction in a low-risk population would require far more patients treated to prevent one event.\\nEducational objective: The number needed to treat is 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction (control event rate minus experimental event rate). Relative risk reduction cannot be used, because it omits baseline risk; the same relative effect yields a much larger number needed to treat in a low-risk population.\",\"wrongExplanations\":{\"0\":\"A value of 1 is the reciprocal of the relative risk reduction of about 92%, a calculation that has no clinical meaning. Relative measures describe proportional change and cannot be inverted into a count of patients treated.\",\"1\":\"A value of 10 is the reciprocal of the control event rate alone (1 / 0.10) and results from forgetting to subtract the event rate in the treated group. That step matters whenever the intervention does not abolish the outcome entirely.\",\"3\":\"The figure 92 is the relative risk reduction expressed as a percentage, that is, the proportional decrease in stroke risk. It is a measure of effect size, not a number of patients, and it would be identical whether baseline stroke risk were 10% or 0.1%.\",\"4\":\"The value 130 is the reciprocal of the event rate in the transfusion group (1 / 0.008) and also happens to equal the group size. It describes how many treated children include one who still had a stroke, not how many must be treated to prevent one.\"},\"eli5\":\"To find how many kids you have to treat to stop one stroke, you look at how much the risk actually dropped in real percentage points, not by how much it dropped proportionally. Ten out of a hundred dropped to about one out of a hundred, so about nine fewer strokes per hundred kids. That works out to roughly one stroke prevented for every eleven treated.\"}]";
